    "seo": {
      "title": "Urban Resilience in Iran: Essential Services and Disaster Preparedness",
      "description": "Explore urban resilience in Iran through hospital safety, multi-hazard preparedness, institutional coordination, and the limits of available evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "resilience-as-a-system",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Keeping a city functioning",
        "content": "Urban resilience asks whether a city can continue serving its residents when ordinary arrangements fail. UN-Habitat, the United Nations agency concerned with human settlements, frames this as a problem of urban planning and management across multiple hazards, including climate-related impacts. Its City Resilience Profiling Programme included Tehran among the cities selected to test and refine assessment tools. Selection demonstrates participation in a programme; it does not establish that a city has achieved a particular level of safety. [s1]\n\nFor readers approaching this subject through climate change, an important distinction is that an earthquake is a geological hazard. Nevertheless, the same hospitals, institutions, and communities may face both earthquakes and weather-related emergencies. Examining their shared vulnerabilities helps explain why urban resilience extends beyond any single environmental threat. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iranian-risk-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A multi-hazard setting",
        "content": "UN-Habitat's Iran project documentation places earthquakes alongside droughts, floods, and landslides in the country's risk context. It also connects urban vulnerability with the growth of informal settlements around major cities. This makes the condition of housing and access to services part of the resilience question, alongside the physical hazard itself. [s2]\n\nThe Emergency Support to Safer Hospitals and Settlements project set out to connect preparedness, response capacity, recovery, and risk reduction. Its stated beneficiaries included healthcare facilities, communities, and vulnerable people. These are complementary concerns: a technically sound facility and a community able to use essential services belong within the same planning framework. The project description establishes intended scope rather than independently measured national results. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "hospital-safety",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Hospitals reveal the practical stakes",
        "content": "UN-Habitat's completion account describes damage to healthcare facilities during the Kermanshah earthquake and earlier disasters, when disrupted hospitals could not perform their expected emergency role. The response included vulnerability studies and demonstrations of retrofitting: modifications intended to improve the performance of existing facilities. [s3]\n\nThe reported work addressed piping, infill walls, and mechanical-system supports. These details matter because a hospital's usefulness depends on more than its main structural frame. The account also describes technical guidance, expert training, and a hospital disaster-risk management software system. Together, these activities illustrate the relationship between physical interventions and the knowledge needed to specify and maintain them. [s3]\n\nImplementation involved UN-Habitat, Iranian public bodies, university specialists, and private consulting engineers, with Japanese support. This is a documented project partnership, rather than a complete map of responsibility for every Iranian city's disaster management. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-progress",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the evidence establishes",
        "content": "The available accounts support a concrete conclusion: resilience work can combine facility assessment, targeted construction, professional training, and coordination between institutions. They do not establish that all hospitals have been upgraded or that nationwide disaster losses have fallen because of these interventions. [s2][s3]\n\nA useful reading of urban resilience therefore separates an assessment from a completed intervention, and a completed intervention from demonstrated performance during a later emergency. For Iran, the hospital examples provide an accessible starting point for understanding that distinction. They should not substitute for city-specific evidence about climate exposure, service reliability, or the needs of individual neighborhoods. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
