    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "A circular economy seeks to retain the value of products and materials through better design, longer use, repair, reuse, and recycling. Iranian waste-management research and plastics-policy submissions illuminate parts of this agenda, but do not establish a comprehensive national measure of circularity.",
    "tags": ["Circular economy", "Resource efficiency", "Reuse", "Recycling", "Product design", "Waste prevention"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T06:39:11Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T06:39:11Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Circular Economy in Iran: Materials, Reuse, and Evidence Gaps",
      "description": "Understand circular-economy principles, evidence from Tehran waste research, Iran's plastics-policy position, and gaps in national measurement."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "retaining-value",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A different way to understand resources",
        "content": "A circular economy concerns the whole life of a product, rather than only the moment it becomes waste. The United Nations Environment Programme, or UNEP, describes circularity through waste prevention, keeping products and materials in use, and restoring natural wealth. This brings design and consumption into the same discussion as collection and treatment. [s1]\n\nUNEP's framework includes repair, refurbishment, remanufacturing, repurposing, and recycling. These terms describe different interventions. Repair keeps an existing product useful; recycling recovers materials after a product has become waste. Treating every activity as recycling can obscure opportunities to preserve a product's function before material recovery is necessary. UNEP identifies buildings, electronics, metals, textiles, plastics, and food systems as relevant sectors. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iranian-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What research from Tehran contributes",
        "content": "A study published in Results in Engineering examined municipal solid-waste management and community participation in Tehran. Its methods combined engagement with waste-management officials and household research in selected districts. The authors emphasized practical support for participation, including training, local environmental organizers, information campaigns, and accessible facilities for storing or exchanging recoverable waste. [s3]\n\nThis is useful evidence about the social organization of material recovery. A household needs more than an instruction to separate its waste: collection arrangements and places to handle separated materials also matter. However, the study's district-based sampling and description of its methods require caution. Its findings should not be presented as a national recycling rate or as proof that the proposed measures have already succeeded throughout Tehran. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "policy-and-outcomes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Policy preferences and measured outcomes",
        "content": "An Iranian delegation submission hosted by UNEP's plastics-negotiation platform emphasizes waste management, recyclability, and the development of recycling capacity. It supports approaches such as simplifying plastic-product design and encouraging recycled material in procurement, while opposing binding obligations on primary polymer production in the text under discussion. These statements document a negotiating position; they do not demonstrate domestic implementation or environmental results. [s4]\n\nThat distinction also explains why circular economy and recycling policy are not interchangeable. UNEP's global plastics framework combines recycling with reuse, redesign, and reducing problematic or unnecessary uses. Evaluating an Iranian programme would therefore require evidence about what it actually changes across those activities, rather than relying on its circular-economy label. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "branches-and-measurement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the branches together",
        "content": "The material-specific branches of this guide—plastic, metal, paper, and electronic waste—sit alongside material reuse and sustainable product design. UNEP's framework helps connect them: product design influences later options, while reuse and repair can retain value before materials enter recycling systems. Shared terminology makes these relationships visible without assuming that each material follows an identical recovery route. [s2]\n\nFor this Iran overview, the inspected evidence remains uneven. It supports discussion of principles, selected Tehran practices, and a documented plastics-policy position. It does not yet support a comprehensive account of regional performance or the scale of Iranian repair, reuse, and remanufacturing industries. Those gaps are consequential: municipal waste evidence alone cannot establish how circular an entire economy has become. [s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
