    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Plastic recycling recovers material from discarded products for further manufacturing. Understanding its role in Iran requires separating documented policy positions from operational evidence, while accounting for sorting, material quality, wastewater, and the limits of recycling alone.",
    "tags": ["Plastic recycling", "Mechanical recycling", "Circular economy", "Wastewater", "Product design", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T06:39:11Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T06:39:11Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Plastic Recycling in Iran: Processes, Policy, and Evidence",
      "description": "Explore plastic-recycling processes, Iran's documented policy position, environmental safeguards, and gaps in verified national recycling data."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "recycling-in-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "One part of a wider plastics system",
        "content": "Recycling gives discarded plastic another material use, but its effectiveness depends on the surrounding product and waste system. UNEP's global plastics framework emphasizes that products should be designed for recycling in the markets where they are sold. A theoretical recovery route is insufficient if the collection arrangements, processing capacity, or market for recovered material are absent. [s1]\n\nUNEP places recycling alongside reuse and changes in product design and delivery. This matters when interpreting claims about Iran: expanding recycling capacity and reducing plastic pollution are related objectives, but they are not identical measures of progress. A programme can be assessed only against its actual scope and results. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-policy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A documented Iranian position",
        "content": "In a submission hosted on UNEP's plastics-negotiation platform, Iran's delegation prioritizes managing plastic-product waste and improving recyclability. It discusses simplified product structures, including single-layer rather than multilayer materials, and encourages post-consumer recycled plastic in procurement. The same submission opposes binding obligations on primary polymer production in the negotiating text. [s2]\n\nThese are policy preferences expressed during negotiations. They should not be read as a verified account of what every Iranian manufacturer produces, what municipalities collect, or what domestic law currently requires. The document is valuable precisely when its evidentiary role remains clear: it shows the delegation's position, rather than independently measuring recycling performance. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mechanical-process",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why sorting and washing matter",
        "content": "Mechanical recycling processes plastic without first converting it into its basic chemical feedstocks. Research on post-consumer polypropylene describes pretreatment involving sorting, shredding, and washing before re-granulation. Polypropylene is a common plastic used in packaging; re-granulation produces material suitable for subsequent processing. Cleaning removes residues that can interfere with processing and product quality. [s3]\n\nWashing introduces an environmental consideration of its own. An experimental study by Michael Süß and Jörg Fischer detected microplastic release during simulated washing of polypropylene. Its findings support attention to wash-water management, but the experiment was not a survey of Iranian facilities. It cannot establish their emissions or justify applying a laboratory result as a national discharge estimate. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "process-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "A simplified mechanical-recycling pretreatment route",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Post-consumer plastic\"] -->|\"Separate suitable material\"| B[\"Sorting\"]\n  B -->|\"Reduce size\"| C[\"Shredding\"]\n  C -->|\"Remove residues\"| D[\"Washing\"]\n  D -->|\"Continue processing\"| E[\"Re-granulation\"]\n  D -->|\"Produces wash effluent\"| F[\"Wastewater requiring management\"]\n```",
        "summary": "Arrows show a simplified material-processing sequence and the wash-effluent stream. This is a general explanatory route, not a verified layout of an Iranian recycling plant.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "local-evidence-and-limits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From household separation to verified recovery",
        "content": "Research in selected Tehran districts emphasizes the importance of training, community support, and accessible waste-handling facilities for participation in waste reduction and separation. This evidence helps explain the collection side of recycling, but does not provide a verified national plastic-recycling rate. [s4]\n\nThe practical distinction is between material identified as potentially recoverable and material demonstrably processed into a usable output. For this page, facility-level throughput, processing losses, end markets, and environmental controls remain unverified. Consequently, no annual recycling chart or nationwide performance claim is presented. The supported account establishes the policy emphasis and explains the process; a fuller account of Iranian operations still requires additional evidence. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
